// Authored conceptual lessons and additional practice. Run after build_content.py.
const fs=require('fs');
const data=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('dist/content.json','utf8'));
const lessons={};
function lesson(id,bigIdea,understand,why,method,example,connection,checks){lessons[id]={bigIdea,understand,why,method,example,connection,checks}}
const M=String.raw;
lesson('1.1','An equation tells you how a quantity changes; a solution is a whole function that obeys that rule.',[
'An algebraic equation usually asks for a number. A differential equation asks for a function because its unknown appears together with derivatives. A proposed solution must work at every point of an interval, not just at the initial point.',
'Order counts the highest derivative. Linearity asks whether the unknown function and its derivatives appear only to the first power, without products between them. A coefficient such as t² is allowed in a linear equation; y², sin(y), and yy′ are not. An initial condition selects a trajectory from a family, when an appropriate uniqueness theorem applies.'
], 'Derivatives encode local information. Integrating reconstructs a function, introducing constants; an order-n regular linear IVP generally needs n initial values to determine them.', ['Identify the independent and dependent variables.','Find the highest derivative and inspect how the unknown enters.','To verify a candidate, calculate the required derivatives, substitute, and check the initial data separately.'],
{prompt:M`Why does \(y=A\cos t+B\sin t\) solve \(y''+y=0\)?`,explanation:M`Two derivatives change each sine/cosine term to its negative, so \(y''=-y\) for every A and B. If y(0)=2 and y′(0)=−1, those data give A=2 and B=−1. Satisfying the ODE gives a family; satisfying the data picks one member.`,math:M`y=2\cos t-\sin t`},'This distinction between a rule and a trajectory returns in phase lines, linear superposition, and PDE modes.',[
['Does y(0)=1 alone prove that y=eᵗ solves y′=2y?', 'No. An initial value checks only one point. For eᵗ the derivative is eᵗ while the right side is 2eᵗ, so the differential equation fails.', 'Check the derivative as well as the initial value.'],
['Why is t²y′+cos(t)y=t linear while y′+y²=t is nonlinear?', 'Coefficients may depend on t. The first equation is linear in y and y′; the second squares the unknown, which breaks linearity.', 'Ask which variable is being squared.']]);
lesson('1.2','Separation works when the time dependence and state dependence can be put on opposite sides.',[
'For y′=f(t)g(y), the change in y can be accumulated by integrating 1/g(y), while the time dependence is accumulated by integrating f(t). This does not always produce an explicit formula: an implicit relation can still describe a valid solution.',
'Before dividing by g(y), find its zeros. They may be constant solutions that the division discards. After solving an IVP, find the largest connected interval containing its initial time on which the original equation and your solution both make sense. Blow-up in a solution and an undefined coefficient are different reasons an interval can end.'
],M`The chain rule says \(\frac{d}{dt}\int^{y(t)}du/g(u)=y'/g(y)=f(t)\). Separation is this identity written in differential notation.`,['Check whether the RHS factors into a time function and a state function.','List any equilibrium solutions before dividing.','Integrate, impose the data, and state the interval containing the initial point.'],
{prompt:M`Solve \(y'=y^2\), y(0)=1.`,explanation:M`Separating gives −1/y=t+C. The data fix C=−1, so y=1/(1−t). The solution becomes infinite at t=1; the IVP interval is (−∞,1). The equilibrium y=0 solves the same ODE but not these initial data.`,math:M`y=\frac1{1-t},\quad t<1`},'The same domain checks matter for integrating factors, Laplace IVPs, and nonlinear substitutions.',[
['What can be lost when solving y′=y(1−y) by dividing by y(1−y)?','The constant solutions y=0 and y=1. They must be listed separately because division assumes the denominator is nonzero.','Set the state factor equal to zero first.'],
['Can y(0)=1 for y′=y² have a solution for all real times?','No. Its unique trajectory is 1/(1−t), which blows up at t=1. Local uniqueness does not imply a global interval.','Inspect the denominator of the IVP solution.']]);
lesson('1.3','A slope field is a map of allowed tangent directions, not a collection of complete solutions.',[
'At a point (t,y), the equation y′=f(t,y) prescribes the slope of any solution passing through that point. A solution curve must remain tangent to these directions as it moves through the plane. Positive slopes mean increasing y, negative slopes mean decreasing y, and zero slopes mean an instant of horizontal motion.',
'If f depends only on y, the field repeats along each horizontal row. If f depends only on t, it repeats vertically. Neither a single arrow nor an isolated zero slope determines long-term behavior. At a singular point where f is undefined, the usual explicit ODE does not prescribe a tangent at all.'
], 'The derivative is the slope of the tangent line. Drawing many short tangent segments turns a local derivative rule into geometric information about trajectories.', ['Evaluate f at a few test points.','Locate zero-slope curves and undefined points.','Follow the directions from the initial point and check whether the qualitative behavior agrees with an analytic solution.'],
{prompt:M`Compare \(y'=y\) with \(y'=-y\).`,explanation:'Both have a horizontal equilibrium at y=0. For y′=y, positive values increase and negative values decrease, moving away from zero. For y′=−y, the directions reverse and trajectories move toward zero. The field tells you stability before you integrate.',math:M`y'=y:\ y=Ce^t;\qquad y'=-y:\ y=Ce^{-t}`},'Collapsing an autonomous slope field into a sign diagram gives the phase line in §1.7.',[
['If y′=t−y, where are the horizontal field segments?','On y=t, because f(t,y)=0 there. This line is a zero-slope locus, not itself a solution: y=t has derivative 1 rather than 0.','A zero-slope locus need not be a trajectory.'],
['Can two solutions of a smooth scalar IVP cross at the same (t,y)?','No, where local uniqueness applies. At a crossing they would share the same initial point, so uniqueness forces them to be the same local solution.','Treat a crossing as a new initial condition.']]);
lesson('1.4','Existence means a trajectory is available; uniqueness means the initial point chooses only one.',[
'For y′=f(t,y), continuity of f near the initial point gives local existence. Continuity of the partial derivative fᵧ is a convenient sufficient condition for local uniqueness. Check a neighborhood, not merely whether a formula happens to have a finite value at one point.',
'These are sufficient conditions. If a hypothesis fails, the theorem is silent; a separate argument is needed to decide what happens. A coefficient undefined at the initial point is more serious: the stated classical equation cannot hold there. Even excellent local behavior does not prevent a solution from blowing up at a later time.'
], 'Continuity permits constructing nearby trajectories; local Lipschitz control in y prevents two nearby trajectories from separating from identical initial data. A continuous fᵧ supplies that control.', ['Write the ODE explicitly as y′=f(t,y).','Locate singularities of f and fᵧ relative to the initial point.','State separately what is guaranteed about existence, uniqueness, and the interval.'],
{prompt:M`Compare \(y'=\sqrt{|y|}\), y(0)=0, with y′=y, y(0)=0.`,explanation:'The first RHS is continuous but not locally Lipschitz at zero. Both the zero solution and a solution that stays zero until time a≥0 and then equals (t−a)²/4 satisfy the same data. For y′=y, fᵧ=1 is continuous; the zero solution is the only one.',math:M`y_a(t)=\begin{cases}0&t\le a\\(t-a)^2/4&t\ge a\end{cases}`},'For regular linear IVPs, coefficient continuity is enough to obtain uniqueness on the coefficient interval.',[
['If fᵧ is not continuous, must uniqueness fail?','No. The derivative criterion is sufficient, not necessary. Its failure removes this guarantee; uniqueness might still be proved in another way.','Distinguish “not guaranteed” from “false.”'],
['Why is checking f(0,1) alone insufficient for the theorem?','The theorem needs continuity on a region around the initial point so nearby states and times are controlled. A single function value says nothing about this local behavior.','Think about nearby initial states.']]);
lesson('1.5','An integrating factor turns a linear equation into a product derivative you can accumulate.',[
'In y′+p(t)y=q(t), the term p(t)y prevents directly integrating y′. Choose a multiplier μ whose own derivative is μp. The two terms then combine into the derivative of μy. This is the product rule used backward, not a formula to memorize without a reason.',
'In a mixing model, track amount rather than concentration first. Amount-in minus amount-out gives the derivative of salt mass. The outgoing concentration is current mass divided by current volume. Unequal flow rates make the volume time dependent, changing the coefficient of the linear equation.'
],M`Because \((\mu y)'=\mu y'+\mu'y\), choose \(\mu'=p\mu\), or \(\mu=e^{\int p\,dt}\). Then integrate \((\mu y)'=\mu q\).`, ['Divide by the leading coefficient to get standard form.','Compute μ and write the product derivative explicitly.','Integrate, solve for y, impose initial data, and keep the model’s physical interval.'],
{prompt:M`Why does \(y'+2y=4\) approach y=2?`,explanation:'The equilibrium balances the forcing and decay: 2y=4. Subtracting the equilibrium, w=y−2 satisfies w′=−2w. Every deviation decays exponentially, so the initial data affect only the transient.',math:M`y=2+(y_0-2)e^{-2t}`},'“Steady response plus transient” is the first-order version of particular plus homogeneous solutions in Chapter 2.',[
['Why is μ=e²ᵗ wrong for 2y′+2y=4?','After dividing by 2, p=1, so a valid integrating factor is eᵗ. Choosing a factor from an unnormalized coefficient fails to match the product rule.','First identify p in y′+py=q.'],
['In a tank, why is the salt-out rate not just the water-out rate?','Water flow has units volume/time; multiply it by mass/volume concentration to get mass/time. The concentration is Y/V for a well-mixed tank.','Check the units of each term.']]);
lesson('1.6','A useful substitution reveals a simpler variable hiding inside a complicated-looking equation.',[
'Exact equations describe level sets of a potential: Mdt+Ndy=dψ. On a simply connected region with the usual continuity assumptions, matching Mᵧ=Nₜ lets you reconstruct ψ. Solutions keep ψ constant, so the implicit curve is often the most natural answer.',
'The other tricks target structure. For a scale-invariant equation y′=F(y/t), set y=tv and remember y′=v+tv′. For a repeated combination y+at, substitute that combination. For Bernoulli y′+py=qyᵃ, use w=y¹⁻ᵃ to make it linear. If y is missing from a second-order equation use v=y′; if t is missing use y′=p(y) and y″=p dp/dy. Always revisit solutions excluded by division.'
], 'Each substitution changes the dependent variable so the chain rule turns a nonlinear or higher-order pattern into separation, a product derivative, or a first-order equation.', ['Classify the pattern before starting algebra.','Write the transformed derivative carefully.','Solve in the new variable, translate back, and check lost constants or forbidden values.'],
{prompt:M`For \(y'+y=y^2\), why use w=1/y?`,explanation:M`On a nonzero branch, multiply by −y⁻² to get w′−w=−1. This is linear: w=1+Ceᵗ and y=1/(1+Ceᵗ). The original equation also has y=0, which the reciprocal substitution excludes.`,math:M`y=\frac1{1+Ce^t}\quad\text{or}\quad y=0`},'Recognizing structure is more valuable than trying every method; this habit carries into forcing trials and eigenfunction selection.',[
['Why must a homogeneous substitution use y′=v+tv′, not just tv′?','Since y=tv(t), both factors depend on t. The product rule contributes the extra v term; omitting it changes the ODE.','Differentiate the substitution before replacing anything.'],
['When y′=p(y), why is y″=p dp/dy?','By the chain rule, d[p(y(t))]/dt=(dp/dy)y′. Replace y′ by p to obtain p dp/dy.','There are two derivatives: one in t and one in y.']]);
lesson('1.7','A phase line predicts where a system moves without needing its explicit formula.',[
'For an autonomous scalar equation y′=f(y), the same state always has the same velocity. Zeros of f are equilibria. Between consecutive zeros, the sign of f is fixed if f is continuous and no other zero occurs, so arrows summarize whether the state moves up or down.',
'An equilibrium is attracting when arrows point toward it from both sides and repelling when they point away. The derivative test f′(y*)<0 or >0 is a shortcut for a simple root. If f′(y*)=0, inspect the signs directly. A parameter can create or destroy equilibria; in harvesting models, a stable and an unstable equilibrium can collide before disappearing.'
],M`Near a simple equilibrium, \(z=y-y_*\) approximately satisfies \(z'=f'(y_*)z\). Its exponential growth or decay explains the derivative test.`, ['Solve f(y)=0.','Test a point in each sign interval and draw arrows.','Classify each equilibrium, then follow the interval containing the initial state.'],
{prompt:'What happens near y=0 for y′=y²?',explanation:'The RHS is positive on both sides. A negative state moves upward toward zero, while a positive state moves upward away from zero. The zero equilibrium attracts on one side and repels on the other; calling it simply stable from f′(0)=0 would be wrong.',math:M`y'=y^2:\quad 0\text{ is semistable}`},'Eigenvalues play a related role in linear systems: they measure growth and decay along particular directions.',[
['Can a trajectory pass through an equilibrium where uniqueness holds?','No. Once it reaches that state it shares the data of the constant solution. Uniqueness forces it to agree locally, so it cannot cross through into another sign interval.','Apply uniqueness at the alleged crossing.'],
['Why can f′(y*)=0 not decide stability?','The linear term vanishes. Higher-order terms may give attraction, repulsion, or one-sided behavior, so the actual signs of f on both sides are needed.','Compare y′=y² with y′=−y³.']]);
lesson('1.8','Numerical methods replace a curved trajectory by small, controlled local approximations.',[
'Euler follows the tangent at the current point for one step. It assumes the slope stays roughly constant during that step, so its local error comes from ignoring curvature. Smaller steps usually improve accuracy, but do not remove questions of stability or model singularities.',
'Improved Euler (Heun) first predicts an endpoint, evaluates a second slope there, then averages the starting and predicted slopes. It better accounts for changing slope. Compare methods at the same final time; the step count and step size must match that target. A numerical value is an approximation, not a new exact analytic solution.'
],M`Taylor expansion gives \(y(t+h)=y(t)+hy'(t)+O(h^2)\). Euler keeps the first two terms; accumulated global error is typically O(h) on a fixed interval under standard smoothness assumptions.`, ['Record tₙ,yₙ and the step h.','For Euler use the old slope; for Heun compute a predictor before averaging.','Advance the time as well as the state, and compare with an exact result or a finer grid when possible.'],
{prompt:'Why can Euler destabilize a decaying solution?',explanation:'For y′=−2y, Euler gives yₙ₊₁=(1−2h)yₙ. The exact solution decays for any positive time, but the discrete multiplier has magnitude greater than 1 when h>1. At h=0.75 it alternates sign while shrinking; at h=1.2 it alternates and grows.',math:M`|1-2h|<1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad0<h<1`},'Stability of discretization is separate from stability of the differential equation; this matters again for numerical PDEs.',[
['Does halving h typically halve Euler’s error at a fixed final time?','Approximately, in its asymptotic error regime for a smooth well-behaved IVP: Euler has first-order global accuracy. This is not an exact identity for every step size.','Distinguish global O(h) from local O(h²).'],
['Why must Heun evaluate the second slope at tₙ+h?','The second slope approximates the derivative at the end of the step, using the Euler-predicted state there. Reusing tₙ misses explicit time dependence.','Both the time and predicted state change.']]);
lesson('2.1','A fundamental set supplies independent directions in the space of solutions.',[
'For a regular second-order homogeneous linear equation, the solution space has dimension two. Two independent solutions are enough to build every solution. Their values and slopes at a time form two vectors; the Wronskian is the determinant that tests whether those vectors span the possible initial data.',
'For solutions of the same regular homogeneous linear ODE on an interval, a Wronskian nonzero at one point stays nonzero, and a zero Wronskian implies dependence. For arbitrary unrelated functions, W≡0 need not imply dependence without extra assumptions. It is the common ODE and uniqueness that make the determinant test so strong.'
],M`The constants satisfy \(\begin{pmatrix}y_1&y_2\\y_1'&y_2'\end{pmatrix}\binom{C_1}{C_2}=\binom{y_0}{v_0}\). A nonzero determinant makes this initial-data system invertible.`, ['Compute the derivatives and the Wronskian.','Check the regular coefficient interval.','Use the independent functions as a basis and solve for coefficients from the data.'],
{prompt:M`Why can \(\cos t,\sin t\) match any y(0),y′(0)?`,explanation:'At t=0 their value/slope vectors are (1,0) and (0,1). They independently control displacement and velocity. At any t, W=cos²t+sin²t=1, so they remain a fundamental pair.',math:M`W(\cos t,\sin t)=1`},'A fundamental matrix is the system version of this same initial-data map.',[
['If one solution is twice another, what initial data are missing?','The two columns lie on one line, so they can represent only a one-dimensional set of value/slope pairs. Two coefficients do not create a second independent direction.','Count directions, not symbols for constants.'],
['Why is checking W at one regular point enough for a common linear ODE?','Abel’s identity gives W(t)=W(t₀)exp(−∫p), with a never-zero exponential factor on the regular interval. A nonzero initial determinant cannot later vanish.','Use the equation satisfied by W.']]);
lesson('2.2','Exponentials turn differentiation into multiplication, so constant-coefficient ODEs become polynomial algebra.',[
'If y=eʳᵗ, each derivative multiplies by r. Substitution produces the characteristic polynomial. Its roots supply independent modes. Real roots give exponential growth or decay; complex conjugate roots combine into real oscillations with an exponential envelope.',
'A repeated root does not give several copies of the same independent function. Multiplying by successive powers of t supplies the missing modes. For a real equation, conjugate pairs should be combined into real sine and cosine terms if real-valued solutions are desired. Count constants at the end: an order-n regular homogeneous equation needs n independent modes.'
],M`Since \(D^ke^{rt}=r^ke^{rt}\), \(P(D)e^{rt}=P(r)e^{rt}\). The root condition P(r)=0 removes the entire exponential factor.`, ['Write and factor P(r).','Record each root and its multiplicity.','Translate roots into a real basis, then impose initial data if supplied.'],
{prompt:'How do the roots −2±3i describe a motion?',explanation:'The real part −2 gives the envelope e⁻²ᵗ. The imaginary part 3 gives angular frequency 3, so the real solutions oscillate with period 2π/3 while their amplitude decays. Frequency and decay are different pieces of the roots.',math:M`y=e^{-2t}(A\cos3t+B\sin3t)`},'These roots become eigenvalues of the equivalent first-order system.',[
['Why are eᵗ and 2eᵗ not enough for the double root r=1?','They are proportional. The second independent mode is teᵗ, so the general family is (A+Bt)eᵗ.','A repeated root requires a new shape, not a new multiplier.'],
['What changes when roots move from −1±2i to +1±2i?','The oscillation frequency remains 2, but the envelope changes from decay e⁻ᵗ to growth eᵗ. The equilibrium’s stability changes.','Separate real and imaginary parts.']]);
lesson('2.3-2.4','One particular solution handles the forcing; the homogeneous family supplies every remaining freedom.',[
'A linear differential operator is a rule that maps a function to a linear combination of its derivatives. Its kernel consists of the homogeneous solutions. If yₚ produces the required forcing f, adding any kernel element leaves that forcing unchanged.',
'More importantly, any two solutions to the same forcing differ by a homogeneous solution: subtract their equations. That proves the family y=yₚ+yₕ is complete. A particular solution need not satisfy the initial data; the homogeneous constants adjust the data afterward. Superposition of forced responses must keep track of their right-hand sides.'
],M`Linearity gives \(L[y_p+y_h]=L[y_p]+L[y_h]=f+0\), and \(L[y-y_p]=f-f=0\).`, ['Write the operator L and identify its homogeneous basis.','Find any one particular solution of the forcing equation.','Add the homogeneous family and then solve for initial constants.'],
{prompt:'If L[y₁]=f and L[y₂]=f, what do their sum and difference satisfy?',explanation:'The sum gives L[y₁+y₂]=2f. The difference gives L[y₁−y₂]=0. This is why adding two solutions to the same nonzero forcing usually does not solve the original problem, but subtracting them identifies the homogeneous freedom.',math:M`L[y_1+y_2]=2f,\qquad L[y_1-y_2]=0`},'Undetermined coefficients and variation of parameters are two different ways to find that one yₚ.',[
['Can there be more than one particular solution?','Yes. Any particular solution plus a homogeneous solution is another particular solution. “Particular” means one chosen solution, not a unique one.','Subtract two candidates.'],
['Why do you impose initial conditions after adding yₕ?','The particular solution may have arbitrary initial values. The homogeneous family supplies the freedom needed to correct those values without changing the forcing.','Separate the forcing task from the initial-data task.']]);
lesson('2.5','Choose a trial family that differentiation keeps inside itself, then fit its coefficients.',[
'Polynomials, exponentials, and sine/cosine combinations have a useful closure property: differentiating does not introduce entirely new function types. That lets you guess a finite-dimensional family for the particular solution and reduce the equation to coefficient matching.',
'The main obstacle is overlap with the homogeneous family. If your trial is already killed by the operator, no coefficient can make it produce a nonzero forcing. Multiply by t enough times to leave the kernel. Even a pure sine forcing normally needs both sine and cosine in the trial because differentiation mixes them.'
], 'Differentiation acts as a linear map on these trial families. Coefficient matching solves that finite linear system. Resonance makes the naive map singular, and the extra t factors repair the trial.', ['Identify the forcing type and include every companion term.','Check for overlap with characteristic roots and multiply by the appropriate t power.','Substitute, match coefficients, and verify the particular result.'],
{prompt:M`Why does \(y''+y=\cos t\) need a t factor?`,explanation:'Both cos t and sin t solve the homogeneous equation. A trial A cos t+B sin t produces zero under D²+1 for all A,B. The trial t(A cos t+B sin t) produces −2A sin t+2B cos t, which can match the forcing.',math:M`y_p=\tfrac12t\sin t`},'The annihilator method explains the t-power correction in terms of root multiplicities.',[
['Why include a constant term when forcing is t²?','Differentiating a quadratic produces linear and constant terms. A general trial At²+Bt+C allows those contributions to cancel and match every power.','Closure under differentiation requires the lower powers.'],
['When is undetermined coefficients a poor choice?','For forcing such as ln t or sec t, repeated differentiation does not stay in a finite polynomial/exponential/trigonometric family. Variation of parameters is more broadly applicable.','Ask whether derivatives generate a finite closed family.']]);
lesson('2.6','An annihilator kills the forcing so you can see which new homogeneous modes a particular solution must use.',[
'A constant-coefficient differential operator can annihilate a forcing function: D³ kills a quadratic, D−a kills eᵃᵗ, and D²+ω² kills a sine/cosine pair. If P(D)y=f, applying an annihilator A(D) gives A(D)P(D)y=0.',
'The enlarged equation has more solutions than the original one. The new modes suggest a particular-solution trial, but you must substitute that trial into the original equation to determine coefficients. Shared roots between P and A increase multiplicity and explain resonance. Different forcing types can be handled with commuting operator factors.'
],M`If A(D)f=0 then \(A(D)P(D)y=0\). Factor multiplicities tell you the enlarged basis; remove the modes already belonging to the kernel of P(D).`, ['Construct a forcing annihilator.','Factor the product operator and identify newly available modes.','Use only those new modes as a trial, and match the original forcing.'],
{prompt:M`For \((D-1)y=e^t\), what is the new mode?`,explanation:'The forcing is annihilated by D−1, so the enlarged equation is (D−1)²y=0. Its modes are eᵗ and teᵗ. The first is already homogeneous for the original equation; the new mode teᵗ gives a particular solution.',math:M`(D-1)(te^t)=e^t`},'This is the operator explanation of the resonance adjustment in §2.5.',[
['Why does solving the enlarged homogeneous equation not finish the original problem?','Applying an annihilator erased information about the forcing. The enlarged family includes functions whose original image is the wrong multiple or combination of forcing terms.','Substitute into P(D)y=f, not only AP(D)y=0.'],
['What annihilates a constant plus sin(2t)?','D(D²+4) is a valid annihilator. One factor kills the constant and the other kills the oscillation; constant-coefficient factors commute.','Use a product for distinct forcing types.']]);
lesson('2.7','Let the homogeneous coefficients vary with time so they can respond to a general forcing.',[
'For a homogeneous pair y₁,y₂, a forced solution can be sought as u₁(t)y₁(t)+u₂(t)y₂(t). Allowing two variable coefficients creates extra freedom, so choose the convenient constraint u₁′y₁+u₂′y₂=0. Then the first derivative has no unwanted u′ terms.',
'The remaining equation is a 2×2 linear system for u₁′ and u₂′, with the Wronskian as determinant. Integrating gives the variable coefficients. Unlike undetermined coefficients, this method does not require a special forcing family, though the resulting integrals may not be elementary.'
],M`For \(y''+py'+qy=f\), solve \(u_1'y_1+u_2'y_2=0\), \(u_1'y_1'+u_2'y_2'=f\). Cramer’s rule gives u₁′=−y₂f/W and u₂′=y₁f/W.`, ['Normalize the leading coefficient to 1.','Choose a fundamental pair and compute W.','Integrate the two coefficient derivatives, construct yₚ, and add yₕ.'],
{prompt:M`For \(y''=f(t)\), what does variation of parameters become?`,explanation:M`Use y₁=1, y₂=t, W=1. Then u₁′=−tf(t), u₂′=f(t). Thus yₚ=−∫tf(t)dt+t∫f(t)dt. Differentiating twice returns f, just as integrating y″ twice would.`,math:M`y_p=-\int t f(t)\,dt+t\int f(t)\,dt`},'The fundamental-matrix formula in §4.3 performs the same construction for systems.',[
['Why can integration constants in u₁,u₂ be omitted when finding one yₚ?','They only add constant multiples of y₁ and y₂, which already belong to yₕ. Omitting them chooses a convenient particular solution.','Identify what those constants multiply.'],
['For 2y″+y=f, can you use f unchanged in the standard formulas?','No. Divide by 2 first; the normalized forcing is f/2 and the homogeneous equation is y″+y/2=0.','The derivative coefficient must be one.']]);
lesson('2.8','The Laplace transform trades a time-domain IVP for an algebraic equation while carrying its initial data.',[
'The transform accumulates a function against the decaying weight e⁻ˢᵗ. For suitable functions this converts differentiation into multiplication by s plus correction terms from the initial values. Solve for Y(s), then identify its inverse as a combination of familiar transforms.',
'A delayed input needs more than attaching an exponential casually. The shift rule is L[H(t−a)g(t−a)]=e⁻ᵃˢG(s). Rewrite the active part in terms of t−a before using it. A transform solution for an IVP starts at t=0; a general time interval from a separation problem is a different kind of description.'
],M`Integration by parts yields \(\mathcal L[y']=sY-y(0)\). Repeating gives \(\mathcal L[y'']=s^2Y-sy(0)-y'(0)\), so the initial data are built into the algebra.`, ['Transform every derivative with its initial-value terms.','Isolate Y(s), factor denominators, and decompose if needed.','Invert using shifts and basic transforms, then check the data and equation.'],
{prompt:M`What does \(e^{-2s}/s\) represent?`,explanation:'Since 1/s is the transform of the constant 1, the factor e⁻²ˢ delays that constant input until t=2. Its inverse is H(t−2), zero before 2 and one afterward. This is a time delay, not multiplication by e⁻²ᵗ.',math:M`\mathcal L^{-1}\{e^{-2s}/s\}=H(t-2)`},'Laplace’s equation in Chapter 7 is a different object: a PDE for a steady spatial field, not this transform.',[
['Why does forgetting −y(0) change the IVP solution?','It replaces the prescribed initial value by zero in the transformed derivative identity. The algebra might still look valid but it solves different initial data.','Derive the boundary term in integration by parts.'],
['How do you transform H(t−a)t for a>0?','Write t=(t−a)+a. Its transform is e⁻ᵃˢ(1/s²+a/s), because the delayed active profile is g(u)=u+a.','Rewrite the profile using elapsed time since the switch.']]);
lesson('3.1.1','An undamped spring converts energy between motion and displacement, producing sustained oscillation.',[
'Displacement from equilibrium creates a restoring force −ky. Newton’s law gives my″+ky=0. The frequency √(k/m) increases with stiffness and decreases with mass. Initial displacement and velocity determine the amplitude and phase, not the natural frequency in this linear model.',
'External forcing can excite a different frequency. Away from resonance, a sinusoidal particular solution has bounded amplitude determined by the frequency difference. At exact natural frequency with no damping, energy keeps being added in phase and the response can grow with time. This is why a bounded forcing need not create a bounded solution.'
],M`A root pair \(r=\pm i\sqrt{k/m}\) gives sine and cosine modes. The conserved energy \(E=\tfrac12m(y')^2+\tfrac12ky^2\) has derivative y′(my″+ky)=0.`, ['Measure displacement from equilibrium and keep consistent units.','Compute ω₀ and the homogeneous oscillation.','For forcing, find a particular response and impose displacement and velocity data on the full solution.'],
{prompt:'What happens if the mass is quadrupled while k stays fixed?',explanation:'The natural frequency is halved because it depends on the inverse square root of mass. The period doubles. This does not by itself determine the amplitude; amplitude still depends on the initial energy and any driving.',math:M`\omega_0=\sqrt{k/m},\qquad T=2\pi\sqrt{m/k}`},'Adding damping changes the energy balance and moves characteristic roots into the left half-plane.',[
['Does a zero initial displacement imply a zero solution?', 'Not if the initial velocity or forcing is nonzero. A spring at equilibrium can move away immediately when given a velocity.', 'A second-order IVP has two initial data.'],
['Why does resonance require a different trial?', 'The forcing frequency matches a homogeneous mode, so the usual sinusoidal trial is annihilated by the ODE operator. A t factor creates the growing resonant response.', 'Compare the forcing frequency with ω₀.']]);
lesson('3.1.2','Damping removes energy; its strength relative to mass and stiffness decides how the motion returns to equilibrium.',[
'The damping force −γy′ opposes motion. For m>0,k>0,γ>0, energy satisfies E′=−γ(y′)², so the unforced system loses energy. Underdamped motion oscillates with a decaying envelope, critical damping has a repeated real root, and overdamping has two real decay rates.',
'The discriminant γ²−4mk compares damping to inertia and stiffness. A large numerical value of γ alone says nothing without m and k. In a driven system, distinguish the decaying transient from the steady sinusoidal response. A forced amplitude peak depends on all three parameters and need not occur exactly at the undamped natural frequency.'
],M`The roots solve mr²+γr+k=0. Their real and imaginary parts encode decay and oscillation; the repeated-root case requires a t factor.`, ['Compute the discriminant and characteristic roots.','Write the appropriate basis, including t for a repeated root.','Use the initial data; for driving, add a particular response and discuss transient versus steady behavior.'],
{prompt:'Why can too much damping slow the return?',explanation:'For γ much larger than √(mk), one root is approximately −γ/m, but the other is approximately −k/γ. The fast transient dies quickly while the slow mode approaches zero more slowly as γ increases. Overdamped does not mean fastest.',math:M`r_{\text{slow}}\approx-k/\gamma`},'An RLC circuit has the same mathematics, with inductance, resistance, and reciprocal capacitance taking the roles of m,γ,k.',[
['Can underdamped motion have a positive damping coefficient?', 'Yes. Underdamped means γ²<4mk, not γ=0. It oscillates while losing energy.', 'Compare γ to the critical value 2√(mk).'],
['What remains after a long time in a damped, sinusoidally driven oscillator?', 'The transient decays for positive m,k,γ, leaving the bounded steady sinusoidal response. The forcing keeps supplying energy, so the motion need not vanish.', 'Separate homogeneous and particular parts.']]);
lesson('3.2','Circuit equations are balance laws: voltage drops add up, and current is the rate of change of charge.',[
'For a series circuit, Kirchhoff’s voltage law combines resistor drop RI, capacitor drop Q/C, and inductor drop LI′. With I=Q′, an RC circuit gives RQ′+Q/C=E(t), while an RLC circuit gives LQ″+RQ′+Q/C=E(t). Charge and current are related, but substituting one for the other without differentiating changes the model.',
'For sinusoidal driving, complex exponentials simplify the calculation because derivatives multiply by iω. Solve the complex amplitude algebra, then take the real part for cosine forcing or imaginary part for sine forcing. The complex phase records a timing shift between the input and response; the physical result is real.'
],M`Substituting \(Q_p=\Re(Ae^{i\omega t})\) gives \(A=[1/C-L\omega^2+iR\omega]^{-1}E_0\) for a cosine voltage of amplitude E₀.`, ['Choose charge or current as the unknown and write the voltage balance.','Check units and initial conditions for that unknown.','Solve transients and steady driving separately; differentiate charge to obtain current.'],
{prompt:'Why does a charged RC circuit approach Q=CE₀ under constant voltage?',explanation:'At steady state the current is zero, so the resistor drop vanishes and Q/C=E₀. Deviations from CE₀ decay with time constant RC. The capacitor charge levels off while the current goes to zero.',math:M`Q=CE_0+(Q_0-CE_0)e^{-t/(RC)}`},'The RLC and damped mass–spring equations share the same roots and forcing methods.',[
['Is Q(t)=I(t) in a circuit?', 'No. Q is charge in coulombs and I=Q′ is current in amperes. Their units differ; one can be constant while the other is zero.', 'Use the physical definition of current.'],
['What does a phasor’s argument represent?', 'It is the phase shift of a sinusoidal response relative to the chosen input. Its magnitude gives the amplitude, while the real or imaginary part reconstructs the physical signal.', 'Write A in polar form before multiplying by eⁱωᵗ.']]);
lesson('8.2','A matrix is a linear transformation; determinants, inverses, and eigenvectors answer different geometric questions about it.',[
'Matrix multiplication composes transformations, so its order matters. The determinant measures signed volume scaling: a zero determinant means some directions collapse and the transformation cannot be undone. For a square matrix, a nonzero determinant guarantees an inverse and one solution to Ax=b for every b.',
'Row reduction explains individual systems, including inconsistent or underdetermined ones. Eigenvectors identify directions the matrix stretches without turning: Av=λv for a nonzero v. Eigenvalues tell you the stretch, but multiplicity in the characteristic polynomial does not guarantee enough independent eigenvectors. That distinction becomes crucial when solving systems of differential equations.'
], 'Linear combinations are preserved: A(au+bv)=aAu+bAv. In a basis of independent eigenvectors, that action becomes separate scalar multiplications, making repeated applications and exponentials simpler.', ['For systems, row-reduce and check pivots and consistency.','For invertibility, compute determinant or identify full rank.','For eigenpairs, solve det(A−λI)=0 and then the nullspace of A−λI; keep only nonzero eigenvectors.'],
{prompt:'Why is a zero eigenvalue a sign of noninvertibility?',explanation:'A nonzero eigenvector v with eigenvalue zero satisfies Av=0. The matrix maps both v and zero to the same result, so no inverse can recover which input was used. Conversely, a square singular matrix has a nontrivial nullspace.',math:M`Av=0,\quad v\ne0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad A\text{ singular}`},'Eigenvectors decouple x′=Ax; the inverse fundamental matrix converts forcing into those coordinates.',[
['Does det A=0 mean Ax=b never has a solution?', 'No. It means no unique inverse exists. Depending on b, a singular system can have no solutions or infinitely many; Ax=0 always has solutions.', 'Check whether b lies in the column space.'],
['Why is the zero vector not an eigenvector?', 'It satisfies A0=λ0 for every λ, so it carries no information about a preserved direction or a specific eigenvalue. The definition requires v≠0.', 'An eigenvector must describe a direction.']]);
lesson('4.1-4.2','A system tracks enough state variables to make the future derivative depend only on the present state and time.',[
'A higher-order scalar equation can be written as a first-order system by storing y and its successive derivatives. For a second-order equation the state (y,y′) records both position and velocity. Two states with the same position but different velocity can evolve differently, so position alone is not a complete state.',
'For x′=A(t)x+f(t), continuous matrix entries and forcing on an interval give a unique solution for each initial vector on that interval. A homogeneous fundamental matrix stores independent solution columns. Unlike scalar multiplication, matrix multiplication is order sensitive, so keep the state a column vector consistently.'
], 'The equation x₁′=x₂ identifies the velocity, and the final state derivative is supplied by the original highest-order equation. No information is lost if the full initial state is preserved.', ['Introduce one state variable for each derivative up to order n−1.','Write each derivative in terms of the state.','Transfer every initial condition to the initial vector and check coefficient regularity.'],
{prompt:M`What state represents \(y''+4y=0\)?`,explanation:'Take x₁=y,x₂=y′. The system x₁′=x₂, x₂′=−4x₁ records how position and velocity interact. Its eigenvalues ±2i agree with the scalar characteristic roots, so both forms describe the same oscillation.',math:M`\mathbf x'=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-4&0\end{pmatrix}\mathbf x`},'This representation makes higher-order ODE methods and matrix system methods two views of the same object.',[
['Why is y alone not a sufficient state for a second-order IVP?', 'The derivative of the state needs y′, and the future depends on both y and y′. Two trajectories can pass through the same position with different velocities.', 'Count the independent initial data.'],
['Must A(t) be invertible for x′=A(t)x to have a unique IVP solution?', 'No. Continuity of A is enough for linear IVP uniqueness. Invertibility of A is not required; A=0 gives the unique constant trajectory for each initial state.', 'Distinguish a derivative rule from solving Ax=b.']]);
lesson('4.3','A fundamental matrix supplies moving coordinates in which forcing can be integrated directly.',[
'The columns of Φ(t) are independent homogeneous solutions. Writing x=Φu describes the state in that evolving basis. When you differentiate, Φ′u already accounts for the unforced dynamics. The remaining motion Φu′ is exactly what the forcing must produce.',
'Thus u′=Φ⁻¹f. Integrate in these coordinates and transform back. Using a definite integral from the initial time automatically keeps the added forced contribution zero at that time. The order Φ⁻¹f matters; it converts the forcing vector into basis coordinates before accumulation.'
],M`Since Φ′=AΦ, substituting x=Φu into x′=Ax+f cancels AΦu and gives Φu′=f. Hence \(x(t)=\Phi(t)[\Phi(t_0)^{-1}x_0+\int_{t_0}^t\Phi(s)^{-1}f(s)ds]\).`, ['Find an invertible fundamental matrix.','Convert forcing with Φ⁻¹(s) and integrate from t₀ to t.','Add the correct initial coordinates and multiply by Φ(t).'],
{prompt:'If A=0, what does the formula become?',explanation:'A fundamental matrix is I, so the formula reduces to x=x₀+∫f(s)ds. Variation of parameters is ordinary integration expressed in the moving coordinates supplied by the homogeneous flow.',math:M`x(t)=x_0+\int_{t_0}^t f(s)\,ds`},'This is the matrix form of scalar variation of parameters in §2.7.',[
['Why does the definite integral begin at the initial time?', 'It vanishes at t=t₀, so the forcing contribution does not disturb the chosen initial vector. The homogeneous part supplies the initial state.', 'Evaluate the formula at t₀.'],
['Can one replace Φ⁻¹f by fΦ⁻¹?', 'Generally no: the first product is matrix times column vector, while the reversed expression may not even be defined. Matrix products also do not generally commute.', 'Check dimensions before rearranging.']]);
lesson('4.4','Eigenvectors identify independent motions; the matrix exponential evolves all of them together.',[
'If Av=λv, then eᵗλv solves x′=Ax. With a full basis of eigenvectors, decompose the initial vector into those directions and let each coefficient grow, decay, or oscillate according to its eigenvalue. Real parts determine exponential stability; imaginary parts describe oscillation.',
'A matrix can have repeated eigenvalues without enough eigenvectors. Then a Jordan block contributes polynomial factors in t, just as repeated scalar roots do. The matrix exponential is defined by its power series even when diagonalization fails. The identity e⁰ᴬ=I guarantees the initial value, and d(eᵗᴬ)/dt=Aeᵗᴬ guarantees the equation.'
],M`If A=PΛP⁻¹, then \(e^{tA}=Pe^{tΛ}P^{-1}\). For a block A=λI+N with Nᵐ=0, the nilpotent exponential terminates: \(e^{tA}=e^{λt}\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}t^jN^j/j!\).`, ['Find eigenvalues and independent eigenvectors.','Use an eigenvector basis if available; otherwise use a Jordan or direct exponential method.','Apply the evolution matrix to the initial vector and verify at t=0.'],
{prompt:'Why can eigenvalue 0 coexist with growth?',explanation:'For A=[[0,1],[0,0]], both eigenvalues are zero, but A²=0 gives eᵗᴬ=I+tA. With initial vector (0,1), the solution is (t,1). A defective zero eigenvalue creates polynomial growth; eigenvalues alone need context at zero real part.',math:M`e^{tA}=\begin{pmatrix}1&t\\0&1\end{pmatrix}`},'Jordan blocks explain why repeated roots generate t factors throughout the course.',[
['Are repeated eigenvalues always an obstacle to diagonalization?', 'No. The identity matrix has a repeated eigenvalue and every direction is an eigenvector. The issue is whether there are enough linearly independent eigenvectors.', 'Compare algebraic and geometric multiplicity.'],
['If all eigenvalues have negative real part, does a Jordan block prevent eventual decay?', 'No. Polynomial factors times decaying exponentials still tend to zero. Jordan structure changes the transient shape but not asymptotic decay in this case.', 'Compare tᵏ with e⁻ᵃᵗ for a>0.']]);
lesson('5.1-5.2','A boundary problem constrains a trajectory at two locations, so compatible endpoint data become a separate question.',[
'An IVP selects a solution from values and derivatives at one point. A BVP selects a solution using conditions at different endpoints. Even when the coefficients are perfectly continuous, those endpoint constraints may be redundant or contradictory, giving infinitely many solutions or none.',
'For a linear BVP, first understand the associated homogeneous problem with homogeneous boundary conditions. If only the zero solution survives, the forced problem has a unique solution under the regular setting in the notes. If a nonzero homogeneous solution survives, a forced problem can have no solution or infinitely many. Adding that homogeneous solution to any one solution preserves both the equation and boundary data.'
], 'After writing a general ODE solution, the boundary conditions become a linear algebra problem for its constants. Rank and consistency determine how many members of the family satisfy the endpoints.', ['Solve the ODE family before imposing boundary data.','Translate endpoints into equations for the constants.','Check whether the equations determine, contradict, or leave free constants.'],
{prompt:M`Why does y″+y=0, y(0)=y(π)=0 have infinitely many solutions?`,explanation:'The first condition sets the cosine coefficient to zero. The second is then automatically satisfied because sin π=0. It adds no new constraint on the sine coefficient; any multiple of sin x survives.',math:M`y=B\sin x`},'Eigenvalue problems ask for parameter values where exactly this nontrivial homogeneous survival occurs.',[
['Does the IVP uniqueness theorem guarantee a unique BVP with continuous coefficients?', 'No. It controls initial data at one point. Constraints at different endpoints can be inconsistent or redundant even for smooth equations.', 'Compare endpoints with an initial value and slope.'],
['If a forced linear BVP has one solution and a nonzero homogeneous solution with zero boundary data, what follows?', 'It has infinitely many solutions: add any constant multiple of the homogeneous solution. Linearity preserves the forcing and endpoints.', 'Use particular plus homogeneous structure.']]);
lesson('5.3','An eigenvalue is a parameter that permits a nonzero shape to satisfy homogeneous boundary constraints.',[
'The zero function satisfies a homogeneous linear boundary problem for every parameter, so it cannot distinguish eigenvalues. We look for nonzero functions. For y″+λy=0, positive, zero, and negative λ produce trigonometric, linear, and hyperbolic shapes, respectively; each case must be checked against the endpoints.',
'Dirichlet conditions usually select sine modes, Neumann conditions cosine modes including a constant zero mode, and mixed conditions half-integer frequencies. A first derivative term can shift the spectrum after a substitution. Do not use a memorized sine formula before examining the actual boundary types.'
], 'Endpoint constraints turn a continuous frequency parameter into discrete choices: for example sin(kL)=0 requires kL=nπ. Only those frequencies close up correctly at both endpoints.', ['Solve separately for λ>0, λ=0, and λ<0.','Apply homogeneous endpoint conditions and require a nonzero coefficient.','List the allowed λ and a corresponding nonzero eigenfunction, including indexing and any zero mode.'],
{prompt:'What changes between y(0)=y(L)=0 and y′(0)=y′(L)=0?',explanation:'Dirichlet conditions remove constants and select sin(nπx/L), n≥1. Neumann conditions select cos(nπx/L), n≥1, but also allow the constant eigenfunction 1 at λ=0. That zero mode later preserves the mean in the insulated heat equation.',math:M`\lambda_n=(n\pi/L)^2`},'These eigenfunctions become the spatial building blocks of Fourier series, heat flow, and waves.',[
['Why must the zero-function solution be excluded?', 'It satisfies the homogeneous equation and endpoints for every λ and carries no spatial mode. An eigenfunction is nonzero by definition.', 'A parameter is special only if a nontrivial shape survives.'],
['Why should λ=0 be checked even when positive-frequency formulas look complete?', 'It can produce a constant mode, particularly with Neumann conditions. Dividing by √λ or assuming a sine family can silently discard it.', 'Solve y″=0 directly.']]);
lesson('6.1','An orthogonal expansion separates a function into noninterfering components, just like perpendicular vector coordinates.',[
'For vectors, the dot product isolates a component along an orthogonal basis vector. For functions, an integral inner product does the same thing. Orthogonality means the cross terms vanish; it does not mean each function has length one.',
'Divide the inner product with a basis function by that function’s squared norm to get its coefficient. A finite expansion is a projection, generally an approximation. A complete infinite basis represents suitable functions in the appropriate convergence sense. At discontinuities, pointwise values require care; a function and its series need not agree at every isolated point.'
],M`If f=Σcₙφₙ and the φₙ are orthogonal, taking ⟨f,φₘ⟩ cancels all n≠m terms, leaving cₘ⟨φₘ,φₘ⟩.`, ['Identify the interval, inner product, and basis.','Compute each numerator and squared norm.','Form the expansion or finite projection, and state its interpretation.'],
{prompt:'Why is the coefficient of sin x on (0,π) not simply ∫f sin x?',explanation:'The squared norm is ∫sin²x dx=π/2, so the coefficient is (2/π)∫f sin x. If you use a unit-normalized basis instead, the denominator becomes 1; those are different conventions.',math:M`c_1=\frac{\int_0^\pi f(x)\sin x\,dx}{\pi/2}`},'Fourier coefficient formulas are projection formulas specialized to sine and cosine eigenfunctions.',[
['Does orthogonal mean orthonormal?', 'No. Orthogonal means different basis elements have zero inner product. Orthonormal additionally means every element has norm 1.', 'Check the denominator in the coefficient formula.'],
['What property characterizes the residual of an orthogonal projection?', 'The residual f−Pf is orthogonal to every basis function used in the projection. Those included coordinates have all been captured.', 'Take its inner product with any included basis element.']]);
lesson('6.2','Sturm–Liouville structure identifies the right weight and boundary conditions for an orthogonal eigenfunction basis.',[
'The form −(py′)′+qy=λry packages a differential operator with a weight r. With regularity, positivity of p and r, and suitable separated self-adjoint boundary conditions, its eigenfunctions for distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal under the weighted inner product ∫fgr.',
'The weight is part of the geometry, not decoration. It appears in both coefficient numerator and norm denominator. Standard sine and cosine expansions are special cases with p=r=1 and q=0. More general coefficients create different spatial modes, but the idea of decomposing into orthogonal eigenfunctions remains the same.'
], 'Multiply two eigenvalue equations by the other eigenfunction and subtract. Integration by parts removes the derivative terms through the boundary conditions, leaving (λ−μ)∫ryλyμ=0.', ['Put the equation in Sturm–Liouville form and identify p,q,r.','Check interval regularity and boundary type.','Use the weighted inner product to project onto eigenfunctions.'],
{prompt:'How is y″+λy=0 a Sturm–Liouville problem?',explanation:'Rewrite it as −y″=λy. Thus p=1,q=0,r=1. Dirichlet or Neumann separated endpoint conditions eliminate the boundary term in the orthogonality proof, leading to the usual sine or cosine basis.',math:M`\langle f,g\rangle=\int_0^L f(x)g(x)\,dx`},'This theorem explains why separation-of-variables modes can be used as an expansion basis.',[
['If the weight is r=x on [1,2], may you omit x in coefficient integrals?', 'No. Orthogonality is with respect to ∫xfg, so omitting the weight uses the wrong coordinate geometry and generally yields wrong coefficients.', 'The norm denominator is weighted too.'],
['Why do boundary conditions matter in the orthogonality argument?', 'Integration by parts creates endpoint terms. The self-adjoint boundary conditions make those terms cancel; without that cancellation the usual orthogonality conclusion may fail.', 'Track the endpoint terms rather than only the ODE.']]);
lesson('6.3','A sine series builds an odd extension and is naturally matched to zero endpoint values.',[
'On (0,L), sine functions sin(nπx/L) form the spatial eigenmodes for homogeneous Dirichlet conditions. Their squared norms are L/2, giving the factor 2/L in each coefficient. You can expand a function that is not zero at the endpoints, but its sine extension then has jumps at those boundaries.',
'The odd extension determines the endpoint behavior. For piecewise smooth data, the series converges to the function at interior continuity points and to the average of the two one-sided values at a jump. Every sine term is zero at 0 and L, so a nonzero original endpoint value cannot be recovered there. This is a convergence issue, not an algebra error.'
],M`Orthogonality isolates \(B_n=(2/L)\int_0^Lf(x)\sin(n\pi x/L)dx\). The shape of the odd extension explains symmetry and jump behavior.`, ['Use the actual interval length L.','Compute sine coefficients, exploiting symmetry or integration by parts.','State the interior expansion and check endpoint or jump interpretations separately.'],
{prompt:'Why does a constant have a nontrivial sine series?',explanation:'A constant on (0,L) becomes positive on one half and negative on the reflected half under odd extension. That square-wave shape requires odd sine harmonics. The sine series is not trying to represent a constant on the entire reflected interval.',math:M`1=\frac4\pi\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\frac{\sin((2j+1)\pi x/L)}{2j+1},\quad0<x<L`},'The same Bₙ become initial-temperature coefficients in Dirichlet heat flow and displacement coefficients in fixed-end waves.',[
['Why is the sine series of 1 equal to 0 at x=0?', 'Every sine term vanishes there. The odd extension jumps from −1 to +1, and its midpoint value is 0. The interior expansion is still correct.', 'Distinguish an open interval from its endpoints.'],
['If L changes, can you keep sin(nx) as the basis?', 'Only when L=π in this convention. The general basis is sin(nπx/L); both the frequency and coefficient normalization depend on L.', 'Fit the zero endpoints to the length.']]);
lesson('6.4','A cosine series builds an even extension and includes a separate constant mean.',[
'Cosine modes have zero derivative at the endpoints and match homogeneous Neumann conditions. Unlike the sine family, they include a constant zero-frequency mode. That mode has squared norm L; the nonconstant cosine modes have squared norm L/2, so their normalization factors differ.',
'These course notes write the series as A₀+ΣAₙcos(nπx/L), with A₀ equal to the mean. Some references write a₀/2 instead; mixing the conventions doubles or halves the constant term. The even extension often avoids a jump at the boundary in function values, though a derivative jump can remain.'
],M`Projection onto 1 gives \(A_0=(1/L)\int_0^Lf\). Projection onto the n≥1 cosine modes gives \(A_n=(2/L)\int_0^Lf\cos(n\pi x/L)dx\).`, ['Write the convention for the constant term.','Compute the mean and higher cosine coefficients separately.','Interpret the even extension and use the correct interval length.'],
{prompt:'What is the cosine series of a constant c?',explanation:'Its mean is c. Every higher cosine integral is zero, so the entire expansion is the single constant term. This is why a Neumann heat solution can retain a nonzero final temperature.',math:M`A_0=c,\quad A_n=0\ (n\ge1)`},'In the insulated heat equation, every nonconstant cosine mode decays while the constant mean persists.',[
['Why does A₀ use 1/L rather than 2/L in these notes?', 'The constant function has squared norm L, while each positive-frequency cosine has squared norm L/2. Projection divides by the relevant norm.', 'Compute ∫1² and ∫cos² separately.'],
['What extension corresponds to a cosine series?', 'Even reflection about x=0, followed by periodic continuation of period 2L. Cosines are even, so the expansion shares that symmetry.', 'Compare cos(−x) with cos x.']]);
lesson('7.1','Heat flow damps spatial variation: sharper modes disappear faster, while boundary conditions decide whether the mean survives.',[
'The heat equation uₜ=σuₓₓ links time change to spatial curvature. A temperature peak has negative curvature and cools; a trough has positive curvature and warms. Separating u=X(x)T(t) produces spatial eigenfunctions and decaying time factors. High frequencies have larger eigenvalues, so fine-scale variation fades fastest.',
'For zero-temperature endpoints use sine modes, which all decay. For insulated endpoints use cosine modes plus the constant zero mode; the average temperature is conserved. Nonzero fixed endpoint temperatures require a steady spatial profile plus a transient satisfying homogeneous boundary conditions. The initial coefficients come from the appropriate Fourier expansion.'
],M`If X″=−λX, substituting XT into uₜ=σuₓₓ gives T′=−σλT, hence \(T=e^{-σλt}\). Boundary conditions select λ and X.`, ['Choose modes from the boundary conditions, subtracting a steady profile if necessary.','Expand the initial transient using those modes.','Multiply each coefficient by its decay factor and check the PDE and all data.'],
{prompt:'Why does sin(3x) decay faster than sin x on (0,π)?',explanation:'Their eigenvalues are 9 and 1. The decay rates are 9σ and σ, respectively. The higher-frequency mode has stronger curvature for the same amplitude, so diffusion smooths it faster.',math:M`u=Be^{-9\sigma t}\sin3x`},'Fourier coefficients describe the starting profile; the PDE prescribes how each coordinate evolves.',[
['Why can an insulated rod approach a nonzero constant?', 'The Neumann zero mode has λ=0 and does not decay. It equals the initial mean, reflecting conservation of total heat in the insulated model.', 'Look for the constant eigenfunction.'],
['For endpoints held at 2 and 5, should the long-time limit be zero?', 'No. The steady profile solves u″=0 with those values, so it is 2+3x/L. Only the deviation from that profile decays.', 'Separate steady boundary data from the transient.']]);
lesson('7.2','A wave mode oscillates in time rather than decaying; its two time coefficients encode displacement and velocity.',[
'For uₜₜ=c²uₓₓ, separation gives the same spatial eigenfunctions as the corresponding boundary eigenproblem, but T″+c²λT=0. Each mode has angular frequency c√λ. Initial displacement determines its cosine-in-time coefficient, and initial velocity determines its sine-in-time coefficient after division by that frequency.',
'Fixed ends use sine modes. In the ideal undamped model there is no exponential decay: energy moves between kinetic and deformation energy. Endpoint-incompatible initial data can still be interpreted using Fourier or weak solutions, but do not define a smooth classical solution at an inconsistent initial corner.'
],M`Write \(T_n=B_n\cos(\omega_nt)+C_n\sin(\omega_nt)\). At t=0, Tₙ=Bₙ and Tₙ′=ωₙCₙ, so a velocity coefficient Gₙ requires Cₙ=Gₙ/ωₙ.`, ['Determine spatial eigenmodes from the endpoints.','Expand both initial displacement and initial velocity.','Attach cosine and sine time factors with the correct frequency normalization.'],
{prompt:'For u(x,0)=0 and uₜ(x,0)=sin x on (0,π), what is the motion?',explanation:'Only the n=1 mode is excited. Its angular frequency is c. Zero displacement removes the cosine-time term; the velocity condition sets the sine-time amplitude to 1/c.',math:M`u(x,t)=\frac1c\sin x\sin(ct)`},'Compare with heat flow: the same spatial basis evolves by decaying exponentials in heat, but oscillatory functions in waves.',[
['Why is the initial-velocity sine-time coefficient not just Gₙ?', 'Differentiating sin(ωₙt) introduces ωₙ. To reproduce velocity coefficient Gₙ at t=0, its amplitude must be Gₙ/ωₙ.', 'Differentiate the modal solution at t=0.'],
['Does increasing wave speed c change the fixed-end spatial mode shapes?', 'No. Boundary geometry determines the sine shapes. Increasing c changes their time frequencies and periods.', 'Separate X(x) from T(t).']]);
lesson('7.3','Laplace’s equation describes a steady spatial balance, determined by boundary values rather than initial time data.',[
'At steady temperature, the time derivative in the heat equation is zero, leaving uₓₓ+uᵧᵧ=0. Curvature in one direction must balance curvature in the other. There is no time variable to evolve, and the boundary data specify the field inside a region.',
'On a rectangle, choose an eigenfunction family in the coordinate with two homogeneous edge conditions. If that coordinate supplies sine modes with X″=−λX, the other factor must satisfy Y″=+λY, giving hyperbolic functions. Normalize that factor so it equals the required coefficient at the nonzero edge. If several edges are nonzero, split into subproblems and superpose.'
],M`Substitution of u=XY gives X″/X+Y″/Y=0. Choosing X″=−λX forces Y″=λY, so trigonometric and hyperbolic behavior must occur together.`, ['Identify which pair of homogeneous edges determines the spatial eigenbasis.','Solve the hyperbolic factor with the remaining homogeneous edge.','Expand the forced edge, normalize each mode, and combine by linearity.'],
{prompt:'Why is sin(nx)sinh(ny) harmonic?',explanation:'Its x second derivative is −n² times itself, while its y second derivative is +n² times itself. The two cancel. A sine in both coordinates would give two negative terms and would not solve Laplace’s equation.',math:M`\Delta[\sin(nx)\sinh(ny)]=0`},'This uses the same boundary eigenfunctions and Fourier projections as heat and wave equations, with a different evolution variable.',[
['Is solving Laplace’s equation the same as taking a Laplace transform?', 'No. Laplace’s equation is a steady spatial PDE, Δu=0. A Laplace transform is an integral technique for time-domain functions and IVPs.', 'Compare the roles of x,y versus t,s.'],
['Why divide a mode by sinh(nH) when the forced edge is y=H?', 'It makes the hyperbolic factor equal to 1 at that edge, so the Fourier coefficient there is reproduced exactly. Without this normalization the boundary amplitude is wrong.', 'Evaluate the proposed mode on the forced edge.']]);

// Add two reason-first exercises to each section, retaining existing problem IDs.
for(const [id,s] of Object.entries(data.sections)){
  if(!lessons[id])throw Error('Missing lesson '+id);
  s.lesson=lessons[id];
  s.problems=s.problems.filter(p=>!p.enriched);
  for(const [i,[prompt,response,hint]] of lessons[id].checks.entries())s.problems.push({id:`${id}-concept-${i+1}`,enriched:true,kind:'concept',ref:'Supplemental • conceptual reasoning',page:s.page,title:i===0?'Explain the idea':'Test a misconception',prompt,hints:[hint],steps:[response],answer:M`\text{Reasoning explained above.}`,check:'A complete response explains why, rather than naming a formula.'});
}
function add(id,title,prompt,hints,steps,answer,check){const s=data.sections[id];s.problems.push({id:`${id}-extra-${s.problems.filter(p=>p.enriched&&p.kind==='calculation').length+1}`,enriched:true,kind:'calculation',ref:'Supplemental • transfer practice',page:s.page,title,prompt,hints,steps,answer,check})}

add('1.1','Classify, then verify',M`Classify \(y''-3y'+2y=0\) and verify y=e²ᵗ. Does it satisfy y(0)=1,y′(0)=2?`,['Compute both derivatives.'],[M`The equation has order 2 and is linear homogeneous. For y=e²ᵗ, y′=2e²ᵗ and y″=4e²ᵗ.`,M`Substitution gives (4−6+2)e²ᵗ=0. The values at 0 are 1 and 2.`],M`y=e^{2t}\text{ satisfies the ODE and data.}`,'The equation and both initial conditions were checked independently.');
add('1.2','Keep the equilibria',M`Solve \(y'=y(1-y)\), y(0)=1/2, and list the constant solutions of the ODE.`,['Use partial fractions 1/[y(1−y)]=1/y+1/(1−y).'],[M`Integration gives ln|y/(1−y)|=t+C. The data give C=0.`,M`Solving gives y=eᵗ/(1+eᵗ). Before dividing, y=0 and y=1 must be recorded.`],M`y=\frac1{1+e^{-t}};\quad y\equiv0,\ y\equiv1\text{ are equilibria.}`,'The IVP solution stays in (0,1) for all real t and tends to 1 as t→∞.');
add('1.3','Read a nonautonomous field',M`For y′=t−y, find the slopes at (0,1) and (2,1), the zero-slope line, and the solution with y(0)=1.`,['Rewrite as y′+y=t and use an integrating factor.'],[M`The slopes are −1 and +1; zero slopes occur on y=t.`,M`A particular solution is t−1; the full solution is y=t−1+Ce⁻ᵗ. The data give C=2.`],M`y=t-1+2e^{-t}`,'The trajectory is not the zero-slope line; substitution gives y′=1−2e⁻ᵗ=t−y.');
add('1.4','A regularity interval',M`For y′=y/(t−2), y(0)=1, give the theorem’s guarantee and solve the IVP on its coefficient interval.`,['The singular coefficient occurs at t=2.'],[M`f and fᵧ=1/(t−2) are continuous near (0,1), so the IVP is locally unique.`,M`Separation gives y=C(t−2); y(0)=1 implies C=−1/2. The regular interval containing 0 is (−∞,2).`],M`y=1-t/2,\quad -\infty<t<2`,'The polynomial extends to 2, but the original explicit RHS is undefined there, so its stated ODE interval stops at 2.');
add('1.5','A decaying forcing',M`Solve y′+y=e⁻²ᵗ, y(0)=0.`,['Multiply by eᵗ, then integrate e⁻ᵗ.'],[M`(eᵗy)′=e⁻ᵗ, so eᵗy=−e⁻ᵗ+C.`,M`At t=0, C=1.`],M`y=e^{-t}-e^{-2t}`,'y(0)=0 and y′+y=e⁻²ᵗ. Both the forcing and the response decay.');
add('1.6','Exactness with initial data',M`Solve (2ty+1)+(t²+2y)y′=0, y(0)=1.`,['Integrate M in t, leaving an unknown function of y.'],[M`Mᵧ=2t=Nₜ, so the form is exact. ψ=t²y+t+g(y).`,M`Matching ψᵧ=t²+g′(y) to N=t²+2y gives g=y². The data set ψ=1.`],M`t^2y+t+y^2=1`,'The differentiated implicit equation recovers the original; the local branch through (0,1) has nonzero N.');
add('1.6','Scale-invariant substitution',M`Solve y′=1+y/t for t>0.`,['Set y=tv, with y′=v+tv′.'],[M`The substitution gives v+tv′=1+v, so v′=1/t.`,M`Thus v=ln t+C and y=t(ln t+C).`],M`y=t\ln t+Ct,\quad t>0`,'Differentiate to get y′=ln t+C+1=1+y/t.');
add('1.6','A repeated linear combination',M`Solve y′=eʸ⁺²ᵗ−2.`,['Let v=y+2t.'],[M`v′=y′+2=eᵛ. Separation gives e⁻ᵛdv=dt.`,M`−e⁻ᵛ=t+C₀, so e⁻ᵛ=C−t. Translate back to y.`],M`y=-2t-\ln(C-t),\quad t<C`,'The condition C−t>0 is required by the logarithm and e⁻ᵛ.');
add('1.6','Missing independent variable',M`Solve y″=(y′)² and include the constant solutions.`,['Either set v=y′ or use y′=p(y).'],[M`With v=y′, v′=v². Nonzero branches satisfy v=−1/(t+C₁).`,M`Integrating gives y=−ln|t+C₁|+C₂. The excluded branch v=0 gives constant y.`],M`y=-\ln|t+C_1|+C_2\quad\text{or}\quad y=C_2`,'Use an interval not containing −C₁. The logarithmic branch has y″=(y′)².');
add('1.7','A zero derivative test',M`Classify y=0 for y′=−y³, and solve with y(0)=1.`,['Test signs on either side instead of relying on f′(0).'],[M`Below 0 the RHS is positive; above 0 it is negative, so both sides move toward 0.`,M`Separating gives 1/(2y²)=t+C. With y(0)=1, choose the positive branch.`],M`y=\frac1{\sqrt{1+2t}},\quad t>-1/2;\quad0\text{ attracting}`,'Although f′(0)=0, the sign analysis proves attraction; the decay is algebraic rather than exponential.');
add('1.8','One step, two methods',M`For y′=−2y, y(0)=1, compute one step with h=0.25 using Euler and Heun, and compare with the exact value.`,['The Euler predictor is 0.5; evaluate the second slope there.'],[M`Euler gives 1+0.25(−2)=0.5.`,M`Heun averages −2 and −1, giving 1+0.25(−3/2)=0.625.`,M`The exact value is e⁻⁰⋅⁵≈0.60653.`],M`y_E=0.5,\qquad y_H=0.625`,'Heun is closer in this example; accuracy and numerical stability still depend on step size.');
add('2.1','Use the basis to fit data',M`For y₁=eᵗ,y₂=e⁻ᵗ, compute W and find the combination with y(0)=3,y′(0)=1.`,['At 0, solve A+B=3 and A−B=1.'],[M`W=eᵗ(−e⁻ᵗ)−eᵗe⁻ᵗ=−2, so the pair is independent.`,M`The initial equations give A=2,B=1.`],M`W=-2,\quad y=2e^t+e^{-t}`,'This is the unique solution of y″−y=0 with the supplied data.');
add('2.2','A repeated-root IVP',M`Solve y″−2y′+y=0, y(0)=1,y′(0)=0.`,['The characteristic polynomial is (r−1)².'],[M`The family is y=(A+Bt)eᵗ.`,M`The data give A=1 and A+B=0, so B=−1.`],M`y=(1-t)e^t`,'The initial derivative is zero even though the solution is not constant.');
add('2.3-2.4','One forcing, many solutions',M`Solve y″−y=2 with y(0)=0,y′(0)=1.`,['A constant particular solution works.'],[M`Take yₚ=−2 and yₕ=Aeᵗ+Be⁻ᵗ.`,M`The data give A+B=2,A−B=1, hence A=3/2,B=1/2.`],M`y=-2+\tfrac32e^t+\tfrac12e^{-t}`,'The constant handles the forcing while the exponential coefficients handle the initial data.');
add('2.5','Polynomial forcing',M`Find a particular solution of y″+3y′+2y=t².`,['Try At²+Bt+C.'],[M`Matching t² gives 2A=1. Matching t gives 6A+2B=0. The constant gives 2A+3B+2C=0.`,M`Thus A=1/2,B=−3/2,C=7/4.`],M`y_p=\tfrac12t^2-\tfrac32t+\tfrac74`,'Substitution cancels linear and constant terms, leaving exactly t².');
add('2.6','Repeated resonance',M`For y″−2y′+y=eᵗ, find an annihilator, the correct trial, and a particular solution.`,['The original operator is already (D−1)².'],[M`The forcing annihilator is D−1, making the enlarged operator (D−1)³. The new mode is t²eᵗ.`,M`Use yₚ=At²eᵗ. Since (D−1)²[eᵗv]=eᵗv″, the output is 2Aeᵗ; A=1/2.`],M`y_p=\tfrac12t^2e^t`,'The resonance correction is t², because the shared root has multiplicity two in the original operator.');
add('2.7','A forcing outside the trial families',M`Find the general solution of y″=1/t for t>0 using variation of parameters with 1,t.`,['The Wronskian is 1; u₁′=−1 and u₂′=1/t.'],[M`Integrating gives u₁=−t,u₂=ln t, so yₚ=−t+t ln t.`,M`Adding A+Bt absorbs the −t term into the homogeneous coefficient.`],M`y=t\ln t+C_1+C_2t,\quad t>0`,'Differentiate twice: y″=1/t. Integrals need not resemble a polynomial trial.');
add('2.8','A switched forcing',M`Solve y′+y=H(t−2), y(0)=0.`,['The transformed equation is (s+1)Y=e⁻²ˢ/s.'],[M`Y=e⁻²ˢ/[s(s+1)]=e⁻²ˢ(1/s−1/(s+1)).`,M`Invert with a delay of 2, including t−2 inside the exponential.`],M`y=H(t-2)[1-e^{-(t-2)}]`,'The solution is zero before 2, continuous at 2, and satisfies the switched equation on each side. Its derivative jumps at the switching time.');
add('3.1.1','Undamped initial motion',M`Solve 2y″+8y=0, y(0)=1,y′(0)=2, and find its period.`,['Divide by mass to get angular frequency 2.'],[M`y=A cos 2t+B sin 2t. The data give A=1,2B=2.`,M`The period is 2π/2=π.`],M`y=\cos2t+\sin2t,\quad T=\pi`,'The amplitude is √2; changing initial data would change amplitude and phase, not frequency.');
add('3.1.2','Underdamped data',M`Solve y″+2y′+5y=0, y(0)=1,y′(0)=0.`,['Roots are −1±2i.'],[M`y=e⁻ᵗ(A cos2t+B sin2t).`,M`At 0, A=1; the derivative gives −A+2B=0, so B=1/2.`],M`y=e^{-t}(\cos2t+\tfrac12\sin2t)`,'The envelope decays and the frequency is 2. The derivative condition fixes B after accounting for the envelope derivative.');
add('3.2','Discharging capacitor',M`For an RC circuit with R=4 Ω,C=0.25 F,E=0 and Q(0)=2 C, find Q and I.`,['The time constant RC=1 s.'],[M`The equation is 4Q′+4Q=0, so Q=2e⁻ᵗ.`,M`Current is I=Q′=−2e⁻ᵗ.`],M`Q=2e^{-t}\ \mathrm C,\quad I=-2e^{-t}\ \mathrm A`,'The negative current means charge leaves the capacitor under the chosen sign convention.');
add('8.2','A singular system',M`For A=\(\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\2&4\end{pmatrix}\), solve Ax=(3,6)ᵀ and decide whether Ax=(3,7)ᵀ is solvable.`,['The second row is twice the first.'],[M`det A=0. For (3,6), the second equation repeats the first: x₁+2x₂=3.`,M`Write x₂=s,x₁=3−2s. For (3,7), the row relation would require 7=6, so it is inconsistent.`],M`x=\binom{3-2s}{s}\ (s\in\mathbb R);\quad\text{second system: no solution}`,'A singular matrix can yield either infinitely many solutions or none, depending on the RHS.');
add('8.2','Inverse of a triangular matrix',M`Find the inverse of \(\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\0&3\end{pmatrix}\).`,['Either row-reduce [A|I] or solve the entries in AB=I.'],[M`The determinant is 6, so the inverse exists.`,M`The 2×2 formula gives (1/6)[[3,−1],[0,2]].`],M`A^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1/2&-1/6\\0&1/3\end{pmatrix}`,'Multiplication in either order gives the identity.');
add('8.2','A defective repeated eigenvalue',M`Find eigenvalues and the eigenvector space of \(A=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\0&2\end{pmatrix}\). Is A diagonalizable?`,['Solve (A−2I)v=0.'],[M`The characteristic polynomial is (λ−2)². The eigenvector equations require v₂=0, with v₁ free.`,M`Only one independent eigenvector exists, so no two-vector eigenbasis is available.`],M`\lambda=2\ (\text{double}),\quad v=a\binom10,\ a\ne0;\quad\text{not diagonalizable}`,'Algebraic multiplicity 2 differs from geometric multiplicity 1.');
add('4.1-4.2','A third-order state',M`Convert y‴+2y″−y=t, with y(0)=1,y′(0)=2,y″(0)=3, into a first-order system.`,['Store y,y′,y″ as three state coordinates.'],[M`Let x₁=y,x₂=y′,x₃=y″. Then x₁′=x₂,x₂′=x₃,x₃′=x₁−2x₃+t.`,M`The initial vector is (1,2,3)ᵀ; all coefficients are continuous.`],M`x'=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\0&0&1\\1&0&-2\end{pmatrix}x+\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\t\end{pmatrix},\quad x(0)=\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\\3\end{pmatrix}`,'Three state coordinates carry the three initial conditions of a third-order IVP.');
add('4.3','Forcing a rotating system',M`Solve x′=\(\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}\)x+(0,1)ᵀ, x(0)=0.`,['Use Φ(t)=[[cos t,sin t],[−sin t,cos t]].'],[M`Φ⁻¹f=(−sin t,cos t)ᵀ. Integrating from 0 gives (cos t−1,sin t)ᵀ.`,M`Multiplying by Φ(t) gives (1−cos t,sin t)ᵀ.`],M`x=\binom{1-\cos t}{\sin t}`,'Its derivative is (sin t,cos t)ᵀ, equal to Ax+f, and its initial vector is zero.');
add('4.4','Two decay directions',M`Solve x′=\(\begin{pmatrix}-2&1\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}\)x, x(0)=(1,1)ᵀ.`,['Solve the second component first and insert it into the first.'],[M`x₂=e⁻ᵗ. Then x₁′+2x₁=e⁻ᵗ.`,M`The family x₁=e⁻ᵗ+Ce⁻²ᵗ and x₁(0)=1 give C=0. Equivalently, (1,1)ᵀ is an eigenvector with λ=−1.`],M`x=e^{-t}\binom11`,'The faster −2 mode has zero coefficient for this particular initial vector.');
add('5.1-5.2','A unique forced BVP',M`Solve y″=2 on [0,1] with y(0)=0,y(1)=3.`,['Integrate twice and apply endpoints.'],[M`The family is y=x²+Ax+B. The left endpoint gives B=0.`,M`The right endpoint gives 1+A=3, so A=2. The associated homogeneous zero-boundary problem has only y=0.`],M`y=x^2+2x`,'Both endpoints and the equation are satisfied; the constants are uniquely determined.');
add('5.3','Dirichlet on a different length',M`Find eigenpairs of y″+λy=0, y(0)=y(2)=0.`,['For λ=k²>0, require sin(2k)=0.'],[M`The left condition removes the cosine coefficient; the right requires k=nπ/2,n≥1.`,M`λ=0 and λ<0 only allow the zero function under these endpoints.`],M`\lambda_n=(n\pi/2)^2,\quad y_n=\sin(n\pi x/2),\quad n\ge1`,'The n=0 formula would produce the zero function and is excluded.');
add('6.1','Project onto constants and a cosine',M`On (0,π), project f(x)=x onto span{1,cos x}.`,['The squared norms are π and π/2.'],[M`The constant coefficient is (1/π)∫x dx=π/2.`,M`Integration by parts gives ∫₀ᵖⁱ x cos x dx=−2, so the cosine coefficient is −4/π.`],M`Pf=\pi/2-\frac4\pi\cos x`,'The residual has zero inner product with 1 and cos x; this is a projection, not an exact identity for x.');
add('6.2','A weighted projection',M`With inner product ⟨f,g⟩=∫₁²x f(x)g(x)dx, project f(x)=x onto the constant function 1.`,['Divide ∫x² by ∫x.'],[M`The numerator is [x³/3]₁²=7/3. The squared norm is [x²/2]₁²=3/2.`,M`The coefficient is (7/3)/(3/2)=14/9.`],M`Pf=14/9`,'The weighted residual integral ∫₁²x(x−14/9)dx is zero. The unweighted average would be 3/2, a different result.');
add('6.3','Sine coefficients of x',M`Find the Fourier sine series for f(x)=x on (0,π).`,['Integrate x sin(nx) by parts.'],[M`Bₙ=(2/π)[−x cos(nx)/n+sin(nx)/n²]₀ᵖⁱ=2(−1)ⁿ⁺¹/n.`,M`Insert these coefficients into ΣBₙ sin(nx).`],M`x=2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n\sin(nx),\quad0<x<\pi`,'At x=π the series is 0, the midpoint value of the periodic odd extension’s jump, rather than the original endpoint value π.');
add('6.4','Cosine coefficients of x',M`Find the Fourier cosine series of f(x)=x on (0,π), using A₀ as the constant term.`,['Compute the mean, then integrate x cos(nx).'],[M`A₀=π/2. For n≥1, ∫x cos(nx)dx=[x sin(nx)/n+cos(nx)/n²].`,M`Thus Aₙ=2[ (−1)ⁿ−1 ]/(πn²); even coefficients vanish.`],M`x=\frac\pi2-\frac4\pi\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\frac{\cos((2j+1)x)}{(2j+1)^2}`,'The series converges to the continuous even extension on [0,π], including both endpoints.');
add('7.1','Nonzero endpoint temperatures',M`Solve uₜ=uₓₓ on 0<x<1 with u(0,t)=2,u(1,t)=5 and u(x,0)=2+3x+sin(πx).`,['Subtract the steady linear profile before expanding.'],[M`The steady profile is S=2+3x. Set v=u−S, giving homogeneous endpoint values and v(x,0)=sin(πx).`,M`That single sine mode decays with rate π².`],M`u=2+3x+e^{-\pi^2t}\sin(\pi x)`,'Both endpoints stay fixed and the long-time limit is the steady linear profile, not zero.');
add('7.2','Both wave initial conditions',M`Solve uₜₜ=4uₓₓ on (0,π), zero endpoints, u(x,0)=sin x, uₜ(x,0)=4sin2x.`,['Here c=2; modal frequencies are 2 and 4.'],[M`The displacement excites n=1 with cos(2t).`,M`The velocity excites n=2 with coefficient 4/ω₂=1 multiplying sin(4t).`],M`u=\sin x\cos2t+\sin2x\sin4t`,'At t=0 the displacement is sin x and the time derivative is 4sin2x.');
add('7.3','A second harmonic edge',M`Solve Δu=0 on 0<x<π,0<y<2 with zero left, right, and bottom edges and u(x,2)=3sin x+sin3x.`,['Each top-edge sine component gets its own normalized hyperbolic factor.'],[M`For n=1 use sin x sinh y/sinh2. For n=3 use sin3x sinh3y/sinh6.`,M`Multiply by the top-edge coefficients and add the modes.`],M`u=3\sin x\frac{\sinh y}{\sinh2}+\sin3x\frac{\sinh3y}{\sinh6}`,'Each mode is harmonic and the normalization gives the exact top-edge values.');

add('1.6','Another scale-invariant equation',M`Solve y′=2y/t for t>0 using y=tv.`,['Use v+tv′=2v.'],[M`After cancellation, tv′=v. The nonzero branches give ln|v|=ln t+C, hence v=At.`,M`The branch v=0 is included by A=0. Translate back with y=tv.`],M`y=At^2,\quad t>0`,'Differentiating gives y′=2At=2y/t. This equation is also separable; more than one method can fit.');
add('1.6','A shifted state and a lost branch',M`Solve y′=(y+t)²−1.`,['Set v=y+t, so v′=v².'],[M`For v≠0, separation gives −1/v=t+C. Thus y=−t−1/(t+C).`,M`The excluded constant branch v=0 gives y=−t, which also satisfies the original equation.`],M`y=-t-\frac1{t+C}\quad\text{or}\quad y=-t`,'Use an interval avoiding −C for the rational branch; substitution verifies both families.');
add('1.6','Bernoulli with a quadratic nonlinearity',M`Solve y′+y=y² and include constant branches.`,['On y≠0 set w=1/y.'],[M`The transformed equation is w′−w=−1, so w=1+Ceᵗ.`,M`Taking the reciprocal gives y=1/(1+Ceᵗ). The lost branch y=0 is separate; C=0 already includes y=1.`],M`y=\frac1{1+Ce^t}\quad\text{or}\quad y=0`,'Restrict to intervals where 1+Ceᵗ is nonzero. Both equilibria 0 and 1 are accounted for.');
add('1.6','Reduce an equation missing y',M`Find all solutions of y″+2y′=0.`,['Set v=y′ and solve a first-order linear homogeneous equation.'],[M`v′+2v=0 gives v=Ae⁻²ᵗ.`,M`Integrating gives y=B−(A/2)e⁻²ᵗ; rename the arbitrary constants.`],M`y=C_1+C_2e^{-2t}`,'The constant mode is retained and there are two independent constants.');
add('1.6','Use y as the intermediate variable',M`Solve y″=−y(y′)² with y(0)=0,y′(0)=1, allowing an implicit answer.`,['Use y′=p(y), so y″=p dp/dy.'],[M`Near the initial point p≠0. The equation becomes dp/dy=−yp. Its solution with p(0)=1 is p=e⁻ʸ²/².`,M`Thus dy/dt=e⁻ʸ²/², or dt=eʸ²/²dy. Integrate from the initial values to obtain the implicit relation.`],M`t=\int_0^{y(t)}e^{s^2/2}\,ds`,'The integral is strictly increasing from −∞ to ∞, so it defines a unique real y for every real t. Differentiating the relation gives y′ and then the original ODE.');
// Put reasoning practice first, while leaving existing IDs and saved work intact.
for(const s of Object.values(data.sections))s.problems.sort((a,b)=>(a.kind==='concept'?0:1)-(b.kind==='concept'?0:1));
data.updated='2026-10-14';
fs.writeFileSync('dist/content.json',JSON.stringify(data,null,2)+'\n');
console.log(`Enriched ${Object.keys(lessons).length} lessons; ${Object.values(data.sections).flatMap(s=>s.problems).length} worked problems; minimum ${Math.min(...Object.values(data.sections).map(s=>s.problems.length))} per topic.`);
